{{-- Breadcrumb trail, on the shared <Breadcrumb>.

    Reads a `crumbs` array from the including page's server block. @include does
    not accept inline data in this version (an `['crumbs' => …]` argument leaves
    the variable undefined and the loop reports "crumbs is not iterable"), and
    the page needs the array declared anyway to drive its @structuredData
    BreadcrumbList from the same source. Each crumb is { label, href?, url? };
    the last has no href because it is the current page. That is the component's
    `items` shape, and the extra `url` the JSON-LD needs is ignored by it.

    Hand-written until now for a reason that is gone: the component loops with
    @foreach(item in displayItems), the parser accepted only the ` as ` form, and
    the directive text shipped literally on all 22 pages. Fixed as of 0.2.395.

    `shell subnav` stays, so the container is unchanged: public/marketing.css
    owns the width, the 28px of top padding and the flex row, and all 22 pages
    that include this extend the marketing layout, so that file is always
    linked. The component's own utilities land on top and resolve to the same
    values here - its links are `fg-muted` (--text-2, what `.subnav a` gave) and
    its separators `fg-soft` (--text-3, what `.sep` gave).

    One thing does change, and it is the reason this was left for a deliberate
    pass. The current crumb was a bare <span> with no class, so it inherited
    `.subnav`'s own `color: var(--text-3)` and rendered QUIETER than the links
    beside it - the one item telling you where you are was the faintest thing in
    the row. That reads as an accident of inheritance rather than a choice. The
    component gives it `text-fg font-medium`, so it is now the most prominent
    crumb, which is what a trail is for. --}}
<Breadcrumb className="shell subnav" :items="crumbs" />
